        {
          "id": "def-generative-lda-and-qda-models",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Gaussian LDA and QDA model contracts",
          "deps": [
            "def-multivariate-normal-law",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $d\\ge1$ and $K\\ge2$ be finite integers, let the class label be $Y\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$, and let $X\\in\\mathbb R^d$. Choose class priors $\\pi_k>0$ with $\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k=1$, means $\\mu_k\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and real symmetric positive-definite covariance matrices $\\Sigma_k\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$. The Gaussian generative model has $\\Pr(Y=k)=\\pi_k$ and conditional laws $X\\mid Y=k\\sim N_d(\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)$. The LDA subfamily imposes one common covariance matrix, $\\Sigma_k=\\Sigma$ for every $k$; the QDA subfamily permits the covariance matrices to differ by class.",
          "strategy": "Specify finite Gaussian classes, positive priors, the shared versus class-specific positive-definite covariance conventions, and the AC premise.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning, 2nd ed., corrected 12th printing",
                "url": "https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1--Wo5Hcl2y_v3DL-tGgcJHRPdtjiVYjS"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stanford CS229 Spring 2022 lecture notes",
                "url": "https://cs229.stanford.edu/lectures-spring2022/main_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-lda-and-qda-log-posterior-scores",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Common-covariance LDA scores are affine and QDA scores are quadratic",
          "deps": [
            "def-linear-score-classifier",
            "def-generative-lda-and-qda-models",
            "thm-nondegenerate-multivariate-normal-law-has-density",
            "def-axiom-of-choice"
          ],
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $d\\ge1$ and $K\\ge2$ be finite, let $\\pi_k>0$ with $\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k=1$, let $\\mu_k\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and let each real symmetric matrix $\\Sigma_k\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$ be positive definite, meaning $v^T\\Sigma_kv>0$ for every nonzero $v$. Define\n$$\nf_k(x)=(2\\pi)^{-d/2}\\det(\\Sigma_k)^{-1/2}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu_k)^T\\Sigma_k^{-1}(x-\\mu_k)\\right),\\qquad p_k(x)=\\frac{\\pi_k f_k(x)}{\\sum_{j=1}^K\\pi_jf_j(x)}.\n$$\nFor LDA, assume $\\Sigma_k=\\Sigma$ for all $k$; then $\\log p_k(x)$ differs by a term independent of $k$ from the affine score\n$$\n\\delta_k^{\\mathrm L}(x)=x^T\\Sigma^{-1}\\mu_k-\\tfrac12\\mu_k^T\\Sigma^{-1}\\mu_k+\\log\\pi_k.\n$$\nFor QDA, allow the $\\Sigma_k$ to vary with $k$; then $\\log p_k(x)$ differs by a term independent of $k$ from the quadratic score\n$$\n\\delta_k^{\\mathrm Q}(x)=-\\tfrac12\\log\\det(\\Sigma_k)-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu_k)^T\\Sigma_k^{-1}(x-\\mu_k)+\\log\\pi_k.\n$$\nThus maximizing the posterior probabilities uses affine scores in LDA and scores of degree at most two in QDA; ties are resolved by the smallest class index.",
          "strategy": "Apply the positive-definite Gaussian density theorem, form the density Bayes ratio, expand its quadratic, and cancel only class-independent terms.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning, 2nd ed., corrected 12th printing",
                "url": "https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1--Wo5Hcl2y_v3DL-tGgcJHRPdtjiVYjS"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stanford CS229 Spring 2022 lecture notes",
                "url": "https://cs229.stanford.edu/lectures-spring2022/main_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "def-binary-logistic-and-multiclass-softmax-models",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Binary logistic and multiclass softmax conditional models",
          "deps": [
            "def-linear-score-classifier"
          ],
          "statement": "For $d\\ge1$, define the sigmoid $\\sigma(t)=(1+e^{-t})^{-1}$. A binary logistic conditional model on $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$ has finite coefficients $\\beta_0\\in\\mathbb R$, $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and specifies $\\Pr(Y=1\\mid x)=\\sigma(\\beta_0+x^T\\beta)$ and $\\Pr(Y=0\\mid x)=1-\\sigma(\\beta_0+x^T\\beta)$ for $Y\\in\\{0,1\\}$. Its classifier predicts $1$ when $\\beta_0+x^T\\beta>0$ and predicts $0$ when $\\beta_0+x^T\\beta\\le0$. A multiclass softmax conditional model has finite ordered labels $1,\\ldots,K$ with $K\\ge2$, affine logits $\\eta_k(x)=a_k+x^Tw_k$ for $a_k\\in\\mathbb R$ and $w_k\\in\\mathbb R^d$, and probabilities $\\Pr(Y=k\\mid x)=e^{\\eta_k(x)}/\\sum_{j=1}^K e^{\\eta_j(x)}$; its classifier chooses the smallest index attaining the largest logit. These models specify conditional label probabilities and impose no distributional assumption on the feature vector $X$, in particular no Gaussian feature law.",
          "strategy": "Define conditional Bernoulli logistic and multiclass softmax probabilities, tie rules, and the absence of a marginal feature-law assumption.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning, 2nd ed., corrected 12th printing",
                "url": "https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1--Wo5Hcl2y_v3DL-tGgcJHRPdtjiVYjS"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stanford CS229 Spring 2022 lecture notes",
                "url": "https://cs229.stanford.edu/lectures-spring2022/main_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "def-binary-logistic-log-likelihood-and-l1-penalty",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Binary logistic log-likelihood and L1-penalized objective",
          "deps": [
            "def-binary-logistic-and-multiclass-softmax-models"
          ],
          "statement": "Fix a finite design with rows $x_i^T\\in\\mathbb R^q$, $1\\le i\\le n$, where $n\\ge1$ and $q\\ge1$. Use either an intercept design, in which the first coordinate of every row is $1$ and $\\beta=(\\beta_0,\\theta)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^{q-1}$, or a no-intercept design, in which no coordinate is reserved as an intercept, the intercept coefficient is fixed at zero, and $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^q$. Let $y_i\\in\\{0,1\\}$ and assume the responses are conditionally independent given the design, with binary logistic probabilities $p_i=\\sigma(x_i^T\\beta)$ for finite coefficients. In either design convention, the log-likelihood is\n$$\n\\ell(\\beta)=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\left[y_i x_i^T\\beta-\\log\\!\\left(1+e^{x_i^T\\beta}\\right)\\right].\n$$\nFor $\\lambda>0$, the L1 objective to minimize in the intercept design is $-\\ell(\\beta)+\\lambda\\|\\theta\\|_1$, so the intercept is unpenalized; in the no-intercept design it is $-\\ell(\\beta)+\\lambda\\|\\beta\\|_1$, so every coefficient is penalized. Here $\\|\\theta\\|_1=\\sum_{j=1}^{q-1}|\\theta_j|$ and $\\|\\beta\\|_1=\\sum_{j=1}^{q}|\\beta_j|$, with an empty sum equal to zero. These are objective definitions and assert no optimizer existence.",
          "strategy": "Write the independent fixed-design Bernoulli log-likelihood and distinguish an unpenalized intercept from the all-coordinate L1 convention.",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning, 2nd ed., corrected 12th printing",
                "url": "https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1--Wo5Hcl2y_v3DL-tGgcJHRPdtjiVYjS"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stanford CS229 Spring 2022 lecture notes",
                "url": "https://cs229.stanford.edu/lectures-spring2022/main_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-binary-logistic-derivatives-and-irls-update",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Binary logistic derivatives and the exact Newton/IRLS update",
          "deps": [
            "def-binary-logistic-and-multiclass-softmax-models",
            "def-binary-logistic-log-likelihood-and-l1-penalty",
            "thm-hessian-characterises-convexity",
            "thm-least-squares-solutions-and-normal-equation",
            "alg-fisher-scoring-irls",
            "thm-irls-is-fisher-scoring-step",
            "thm-glm-general-link-score-and-information",
            "def-generalized-linear-model",
            "def-glm-observation-family"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $n,q\\ge1$ be finite, let $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times q}$ be a fixed design matrix whose first column is the intercept column of ones, and let $y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$. For finite $\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^q$, put $\\eta=X\\beta$, $p_i=\\sigma(\\eta_i)$, $W=\\operatorname{diag}(p_i(1-p_i))$, and $\\ell(\\beta)=\\sum_i[y_i\\eta_i-\\log(1+e^{\\eta_i})]$. Then\n$$\n\\nabla\\ell(\\beta)=X^T(y-p),\\qquad \\nabla^2\\ell(\\beta)=-X^TWX.\n$$\nThe diagonal entries of $W$ are positive, so $-\\ell$ is convex; if $X$ has full column rank, $X^TWX$ is positive definite. In that full-rank case, the exact Newton step for maximizing the unpenalized log-likelihood is\n$$\n\\beta_{\\mathrm{new}}=\\beta+(X^TWX)^{-1}X^T(y-p)=(X^TWX)^{-1}X^TWz,\\qquad z=X\\beta+W^{-1}(y-p).\n$$\nThis is the Bernoulli-logit IRLS step with the weighted least-squares convention using weights $p_i(1-p_i)$. The identities describe one update and do not assert convergence of repeated updates.",
          "strategy": "Differentiate the scalar likelihood, establish W positivity and rank conditions, verify the Bernoulli GLM hypotheses, and identify the weighted least-squares IRLS output with the Newton solve.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning, 2nd ed., corrected 12th printing",
                "url": "https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1--Wo5Hcl2y_v3DL-tGgcJHRPdtjiVYjS"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stanford CS229 Spring 2022 lecture notes",
                "url": "https://cs229.stanford.edu/lectures-spring2022/main_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "justified_by": []
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-complete-separation-precludes-finite-logistic-mle",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Complete separation gives an unattained logistic likelihood supremum",
          "deps": [
            "def-binary-logistic-and-multiclass-softmax-models",
            "def-binary-logistic-log-likelihood-and-l1-penalty"
          ],
          "statement": "Let $n,q\\ge1$ be finite, let $x_i\\in\\mathbb R^q$ be a fixed design row with an intercept coordinate when one is used, and let $y_i\\in\\{0,1\\}$. Suppose the unpenalized binary logistic log-likelihood is $\\ell(\\beta)=\\sum_i[y_i x_i^T\\beta-\\log(1+e^{x_i^T\\beta})]$. If there is a vector $v\\in\\mathbb R^q$ such that $(2y_i-1)x_i^Tv>0$ for every $i$, then $\\sup_{\\beta\\in\\mathbb R^q}\\ell(\\beta)=0$, and no finite coefficient vector attains this supremum.",
          "strategy": "Scale a strict separating vector to make each likelihood term approach zero, then show every finite coefficient has strictly negative likelihood.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Hastie, Tibshirani, and Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning, 2nd ed., corrected 12th printing",
                "url": "https://drive.google.com/uc?export=download&id=1--Wo5Hcl2y_v3DL-tGgcJHRPdtjiVYjS"
              },
              {
                "title": "Stanford CS229 Spring 2022 lecture notes",
                "url": "https://cs229.stanford.edu/lectures-spring2022/main_notes.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "provenance": {
