          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a set, let $m:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R$, and let\n$k:\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R$ be symmetric and positive\nsemidefinite, meaning that for every finite ordered list $t_1,\\ldots,t_r$\nin $\\mathcal X$ and every $a\\in\\mathbb R^r$,\n$$\\sum_{i,j=1}^r a_i a_j k(t_i,t_j)\\ge0.$$\nFor finite ordered lists $A=(a_i)_{i=1}^r$ and $B=(b_j)_{j=1}^s$ in\n$\\mathcal X$, define\n$$K_{AB}=(k(a_i,b_j))_{i,j};$$\nrepeated indices are allowed.\n\nGiven a real Gaussian process $f=(f_t)_{t\\in\\mathcal X}$ with mean\n$m(t)=\\mathbb E[f_t]$ and covariance\n$k(s,t)=\\mathbb E[(f_s-m(s))(f_t-m(t))]$, and a nonempty finite training list\n$X=(x_i)_{i=1}^n$ in $\\mathcal X$, define $f_X=(f_{x_i})_{i=1}^n$ and\n$m_X=(m(x_i))_{i=1}^n$. For a centered Gaussian noise vector\n$\\varepsilon\\sim N_n(0,R)$ with symmetric positive-semidefinite covariance\n$R$ that is independent of $f$, define the observation vector\n$Y=f_X+\\varepsilon$ and the matrix\n$A_X=K_{XX}+R$.\n\nFor a nonempty finite test list $X_*=(x_j^*)_{j=1}^p$ in $\\mathcal X$,\ndefine the latent test\nvector $f_*=f_{X_*}$. If a centered Gaussian vector\n$\\eta_*\\sim N_p(0,R_*)$ with symmetric positive-semidefinite covariance\n$R_*$ is independent of the pair $(f,\\varepsilon)$, define the noisy test\nvector $Y_*=f_*+\\eta_*$.",
