      "id": "ml02-clustering-mixtures",
      "title": "K-means, Gaussian mixtures, and soft assignments",
      "kind": "A",
      "category": "machine-learning",
      "label": "ML-19",
      "companion": "ml02-clustering-mixtures-examples",
      "requires": [
        "central-limit-theorems",
        "em-algorithm-and-latent-variable-likelihood-ascent",
        "independence-borel-cantelli-and-zero-one-laws",
        "inner-product-spaces-and-orthogonality",
        "lebesgue-measure-on-euclidean-space",
        "numerical-optimization-for-statistical-computation",
        "probability-spaces-random-variables-and-expectation",
        "product-measures-and-the-fubini-tonelli-theorems",
        "the-spectral-theorem-and-singular-value-decomposition",
        "weak-convergence-tightness-and-representation"
      ],
      "description": "Controlling refreshed prose: research/plan-machine-learning-track.md, complete pair section and Current published supplier reconciliation (SHA-256 348a19b0017653faf85a3708273ef33461a42a3d1dffb0e3861684b7f53fff73). Read its complete current contract and referenced prerequisite modules; preserved historical wording does not override the dated interface repair. This is prose planning, not proof acceptance.\n\n**2026-10-18 interface repair:** Reuse published `alg-expectation-maximization`, `thm-em-observed-log-likelihood-is-nondecreasing`, `thm-generalized-em-is-nondecreasing-under-q-ascent`, and the published KL definition/nonnegativity. Do not mint a second generic dominated KL or EM-ascent theorem. The exact support, log-integrability, finite-positive observed normalizers, finite KL and AC clauses in the current supplier must be checked for each Gaussian-mixture step. The local affine-span/SPD covariance proof, collapse counterexample and Lloyd tie-sensitive termination remain ML-owned.\n\n**Pages:** ml02-clustering-mixtures / ml02-clustering-mixtures-examples. **Scope:** For $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb R^p$ and $1\\le K\\le n$, define labels $z_i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}$, centers $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_K$, and $J(z,\\mu)=\\sum_i\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}\\|_2^2$. Initialize labels by nearest-center assignment with smallest-index ties. At each Lloyd iteration assign each point to a nearest center (retain its current label on a tie, otherwise choose the smallest index), replace every nonempty cluster center by its sample mean, and leave an empty-cluster center unchanged. This tie rule makes any changed assignment strictly reduce the fixed-center objective; combined with mean minimization and finitely many assignments, the procedure terminates at a coordinatewise fixed point. It may reach different local fixed points from different starts; global partition search is combinatorial. For fixed positive mixture weights $\\pi_k$ and centers $\\mu_k$, equal spherical Gaussian components $N(\\mu_k,\\sigma^2I)$ have responsibilities proportional to $\\pi_k\\exp[-\\|x_i-\\mu_k\\|^2/(2\\sigma^2)]$; as $\\sigma\\downarrow0$, a unique nearest center receives responsibility tending to one, and tied nearest centers divide limiting responsibility in proportion to their $\\pi_k$. The finite-data objective satisfies $-2\\sigma^2\\sum_i\\log\\sum_k\\pi_k\\exp[-\\|x_i-\\mu_k\\|^2/(2\\sigma^2)]\\to\\sum_i\\min_k\\|x_i-\\mu_k\\|^2$. This is a fixed-parameter low-variance limit, not a claim that EM iterates converge to a global K-means solution.\n\nDefine $\\phi_p(x;\\mu,\\Sigma)=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}(\\det\\Sigma)^{-1/2}\\exp[-(x-\\mu)^T\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)/2]$ for $\\Sigma\\succ0$, and locally prove it is a normalized density using the positive spectral square root and linear change of variables from independent scalar standard normals. Define a $K$-component Gaussian mixture with $\\pi_k>0$, $\\sum_k\\pi_k=1$, means $\\mu_k\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and SPD covariances $\\Sigma_k$. For fixed observations $x_i$, define responsibilities $\\gamma_{ik}=\\frac{\\pi_k\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_k,\\Sigma_k)}{\\sum_j\\pi_j\\phi_p(x_i;\\mu_j,\\Sigma_j)}$ and exact EM updates $N_k=\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}$, $\\pi_k'=N_k/n$, $\\mu_k'=N_k^{-1}\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}x_i$, $\\Sigma_k'=N_k^{-1}\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}(x_i-\\mu_k')(x_i-\\mu_k')^T$. For the closed-form unrestricted M-step to stay inside the stated SPD domain, require the observed finite points affinely span $\\mathbb R^p$; otherwise use an explicitly constrained covariance domain and its corresponding constrained M-step. For $K\\ge2$, the unrestricted Gaussian-mixture likelihood can be unbounded as one component covariance collapses onto a data point while another component explains the remaining observations; no global maximum or parameter convergence follows.\n\nFor a general dominated latent model, define observed density $p_\\theta(x)=\\int p_\\theta(x,z)\\,d\\nu(z)$ and log likelihood $\\ell(\\theta;x)=\\log p_\\theta(x)$. Require $0<p_{\\theta_t}(x)<\\infty$, posterior density $q_t(z)=p_{\\theta_t}(x,z)/p_{\\theta_t}(x)$, common posterior support $q_t\\ll p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid x)$ for candidate updates, and finite expectations/KL in the identity. Define $D_{KL}(q\\|p)=\\int q(z)\\log(q(z)/p(z))\\,d\\nu(z)$ when $q\\ll p$ and the log integral is finite; prove nonnegativity by writing $q=rp$ and Jensen for convex $r\\mapsto r\\log r$ under probability density $p$. Then $\\ell(\\theta;x)-\\ell(\\theta_t;x)=Q(\\theta,\\theta_t)-Q(\\theta_t,\\theta_t)+D_{KL}(q_t\\|p_\\theta(\\cdot\\mid x))$, where $Q(\\theta,\\theta_t)=E_{q_t}\\log p_\\theta(x,Z)$. Any generalized M-step with $Q(\\theta_{t+1},\\theta_t)\\ge Q(\\theta_t,\\theta_t)$ therefore has nondecreasing observed log likelihood.\n\n**Proof route:** For K-means, expand square loss about each nonempty-cluster mean and use pointwise nearest assignment; prove strict decrease on changed assignments under the tie rule, so a finite assignment space gives termination. For the Gaussian responsibilities/M-step, derive Bayes’ formula and weighted normal maximum-likelihood updates; verify SPD using positive responsibilities and affine full span. The M-step proof expands the weighted complete log likelihood, maximizes the simplex term at $\\pi_k=N_k/n$, the weighted quadratic at $\\mu_k=\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}x_i/N_k$, and the covariance term after writing $A_k=\\Sigma_k^{-1}$ as $N_k\\log\\det A_k/2-\\operatorname{tr}(A_kS_k)/2$. Prove strict concavity of $\\log\\det A$ on SPD matrices from $D^2\\log\\det(A)[H,H]=-\\operatorname{tr}(A^{-1}HA^{-1}H)<0$ for symmetric nonzero $H$; the unique covariance maximizer is $A_k=N_kS_k^{-1}$. Prove EM monotonicity by the displayed KL decomposition (or the Jensen/ELBO equivalent), with all support and integrability conditions; do not infer convergence of parameters from likelihood monotonicity. The low-variance limit follows by factoring the minimum squared distance from the finite log-sum-exp and taking $\\sigma\\to0$.\n\n**Examples:** Initialization-dependent two-cluster data; fractional responsibilities for overlapping components; collapsed covariance and unbounded likelihood; tied nearest centers.\n\n**Requires:** published `thm-least-squares-solutions-and-normal-equation` (finite-dimensional least squares), `def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable`, `def-moments-variance-and-covariance` (when variance/covariance is used), `thm-jensen-inequality-for-expectation` with its integrability/nonnegative-composition premise, `def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws` and `def-multivariate-normal-law` (both assume AC), `thm-independent-random-elements-have-product-joint-law`, `thm-tonelli-theorem-for-sigma-finite-product-spaces`, `cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms`, and `thm-linear-change-of-variables-for-lebesgue-measure` (assumes Countable Choice, hence AC suffices), with their exact scalar/product, finite-dimensional, and measure-change clauses. These suppliers only support the local SPD Gaussian-density normalization proof; singular Gaussian laws are outside the mixture density formula. The generic EM proof is supplied by published Category 3; only the Gaussian-mixture/Lloyd specializations are local, and no Category 4 pair is an upstream dependency of Cat3. **Sources:** ESL §§13.2.1–13.2.3, printed pp. 460–463; Exercise 13.1, printed p. 481 (which poses, but does not prove, the low-variance limit); §§14.3.4–14.3.7, pp. 507–512; §8.5, pp. 272–281. ESL gives K-means descent and Gaussian mixture responsibilities, and its general EM monotonicity derivation (8.44)–(8.47); the present common-support/integrability and finite-stopping contracts are explicit local completions. Proposed items: `def-k-means-objective`; `alg-lloyd-k-means`; `thm-lloyd-objective-monotonicity-and-termination`; `def-finite-gaussian-mixture`; `alg-gaussian-mixture-em`; `def-dominated-kl-divergence-for-em`; `lem-log-determinant-strictly-concave-on-positive-definite-matrices`; `thm-em-observed-likelihood-monotonicity-under-common-support`; `thm-equal-spherical-mixture-low-variance-kmeans-limit`. The source does not provide a general parameter-convergence theorem.",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "def-k-means-objective",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "K-means objective",
          "statement": "Let $n,p$ be positive integers, let $1\\le K\\le n$, let $x_1,\\ldots,x_n\\in\\mathbb R^p$ be observed vectors, and let $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_K\\in\\mathbb R^p$ be centers. A labeling is a vector $z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}^n$, and its $k$th cluster is $C_k(z):=\\{i:z_i=k\\}$. For $v=(v_1,\\ldots,v_p)\\in\\mathbb R^p$, set $\\|v\\|_2^2:=\\sum_{j=1}^p v_j^2$. The K-means objective is\n\n$$J(z,\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_K):=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\|x_i-\\mu_{z_i}\\|_2^2=\\sum_{k=1}^K\\sum_{i\\in C_k(z)}\\|x_i-\\mu_k\\|_2^2.$$\n\nAn empty cluster contributes zero to the sum.",
