olates). Define unnormalized/normalized spectral clustering as graph construction, eigenvector embedding, optional row normalization in the normalized variant, and a deterministic K-means step. The relaxation gives a lower bound on the combinatorial optimum, not a rounding or approximation guarantee. Graph weights, bandwidth, kNN symmetrization, degree handling and cluster count are modeling choices. **Proof route:** derive the weighted quadratic identity and kernel characterization locally; derive the indicator trace formulas; prove the finite-dimensional trace-minimization eigenvector lemma by spectral decomposition. The published simple unweighted Laplacian results apply only to the equal-positive-weight special case; they do not prove the arbitrary weighted extension. Davis–Kahan perturbation bounds require an eigengap and a specified matrix norm and need a local proof or exact supplier. **Examples:** disconnected positive-weight components; an isolate; a kNN graph where changing symmetrization changes the cut; concentric-circle embeddings. **Requires:** published `def-laplacian-matrix-of-a-finite-simple-graph`, `thm-laplacian-is-positive-semidefinite-and-annihilates-ones`, and `thm-laplacian-kernel-dimension-counts-components` only for the unweighted special case; `cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms` for finite symmetric eigen-decompositions; `cor-rayleigh-quotient-extreme-eigenvalue-characterisation` for the one-vector case; ML-19 — K-means, Gaussian mixtures, and soft assignments for the final rounding algorithm. Prove the multi-vector trace-minimization lemma locally; the extreme-Rayleigh corollary alone is insufficient. **Sources:** von Luxburg, “A Tutorial on Spectral Clustering,” §§2–5, printed pp. 4–22, especially the RatioCut/Ncut indicator identities and relaxations in §§5.1–5.3; Theorem 7 (Davis–Kahan) in §7.1 is stated but referred to matrix perturbation sources for proof. ESL §14.5.3, printed pp. 544–547, gives the algorithmic comparison. Neither source proves graph/manifold consistency.",
