      ],
      "description": "Controlling refreshed prose: research/plan-machine-learning-track.md, complete pair section and Current published supplier reconciliation (SHA-256 348a19b0017653faf85a3708273ef33461a42a3d1dffb0e3861684b7f53fff73). Read its complete current contract and referenced prerequisite modules; preserved historical wording does not override the dated interface repair. This is prose planning, not proof acceptance.\n\n**Pages:** ml02-latent-representations / ml02-latent-representations-examples. **Scope:** Define the covariance factor model with $X\\in\\mathbb R^p$, $S\\in\\mathbb R^q$, $A\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times q}$ and $q<p$, as $X=AS+\\varepsilon$, $E[S]=E[\\varepsilon]=0$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(S)=I_q$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\varepsilon)=D$ diagonal positive, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(S,\\varepsilon)=0$, so $\\operatorname{Cov}(X)=AA^T+D$. Prove covariance nonidentifiability under $A\\mapsto AR^T$, $S\\mapsto RS$ for every orthogonal $R$; this is a covariance-model statement (and an exact law symmetry when the standardized factors are jointly Gaussian), not identification of scientific causes.\n\nDefine square ICA by $X,S\\in\\mathbb R^p$ and $X=AS$ with $A\\in GL_p(\\mathbb R)$, mutually independent centered sources of unit variance, and finite second moments. State the classical identifiability condition “at most one source is Gaussian” and the resulting permutation/sign ambiguity after unit-variance standardization (or scale/permutation ambiguity without standardization). Whitening reduces the remaining mixing ambiguity to an orthogonal transform. ESL states this condition but does not prove the Darmois–Skitovich/characteristic-function theorem, so the identifiability result remains a local proof obligation unless a complete authoritative proof is supplied.\n\nFor Poisson NMF, take count data $x_{ij}\\in\\mathbb N_0$ with no all-zero row or column (otherwise remove it or specify a separate zero-factor rule), factors $W\\in[0,\\infty)^{n\\times r}$ and $H\\in[0,\\infty)^{r\\times p}$, rates $\\lambda_{ij}=(WH)_{ij}$, and independent $X_{ij}\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(\\lambda_{ij})$. Define the log-likelihood up to constants as $\\ell(W,H)=\\sum_{ij}[x_{ij}\\log\\lambda_{ij}-\\lambda_{ij}]$, with $0\\log0=0$ and $\\ell=-\\infty$ if $x_{ij}>0=\\lambda_{ij}$. For positive initialization and strictly positive denominators, define the source’s multiplicative updates $W_{ik}\\leftarrow W_{ik}\\frac{\\sum_jH_{kj}x_{ij}/(WH)_{ij}}{\\sum_jH_{kj}}$ and $H_{kj}\\leftarrow H_{kj}\\frac{\\sum_iW_{ik}x_{ij}/(WH)_{ij}}{\\sum_iW_{ik}}$. Positive component rescalings $W_{\\cdot k}\\mapsto cW_{\\cdot k}$, $H_{k\\cdot}\\mapsto c^{-1}H_{k\\cdot}$ and component permutations leave $WH$ unchanged; additional nonuniqueness can occur. The factor objective is nonconvex, and no global optimum or convergence claim is made. **Proof route:** prove the factor covariance invariance by substitution. Prove ICA identifiability locally only if the independence theorem and its regularity conditions are supplied. Derive the Poisson likelihood directly. ESL assigns the multiplicative-update minorization proof to Exercise 14.23; author the full auxiliary-function/MM proof before claiming likelihood monotonicity, or present the update only as a finite procedure with no convergence claim. **Examples:** rotate two standardized Gaussian factors; non-Gaussian independent sources with permutation/sign ambiguity; nonunique nonnegative cones; positive NMF initialization and zero-count entries. **Requires:** published `thm-singular-value-decomposition` and `cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms` for whitening/eigen-algebra only. Cat3 latent likelihood/EM interfaces are optional for a separately specified probabilistic fit. The ICA and NMF guarantees are local proof obligations; do not turn source conditions or citations into proof evidence. **Sources:** ESL §§14.7.1–14.7.2, printed pp. 558–565 (factor analysis/ICA assumptions and discussion), and §14.6, pp. 553–554 (Poisson NMF objective/update; Exercise 14.23 leaves its MM proof open). ESL explains the ICA non-Gaussian condition but does not prove identifiability.",
