{
      "id": "ml02-metric-learning",
      "title": "Nearest neighbors and learned metrics",
      "kind": "A",
      "category": "machine-learning",
      "label": "ML-23",
      "companion": "ml02-metric-learning-examples",
      "requires": [
        "countability-and-uncountability",
        "hilbert-space-geometry-and-riesz-representation",
        "matrix-norms-condition-numbers-and-numerical-stability",
        "subgradient-bundle-and-derivative-free-optimization",
        "the-spectral-theorem-and-singular-value-decomposition"
      ],
      "description": "Controlling refreshed prose: research/plan-machine-learning-track.md, complete pair section and Current published supplier reconciliation (SHA-256 348a19b0017653faf85a3708273ef33461a42a3d1dffb0e3861684b7f53fff73). Read its complete current contract and referenced prerequisite modules; preserved historical wording does not override the dated interface repair. This is prose planning, not proof acceptance.\n\n**2026-10-18 interface repair:** Reuse published `alg-projected-subgradient-method` and `thm-projected-subgradient-best-and-average-bound` when applying a finite-budget convex solver to the fixed-neighbor LMNN objective. A global convergence rate additionally needs an attained comparator and the theorem’s bounded subgradients on the iterate domain; convexity alone is insufficient. Eigenvalue clipping remains the exact finite PSD projection specialization.\n\n**Pages:** ml02-metric-learning / ml02-metric-learning-examples. **Scope:** For a finite labeled sample $(x_i,y_i)_{i=1}^n$ in $\\mathbb R^d\\times\\{1,\\ldots,C\\}$, define kNN using $1\\le k\\le n$ and a fixed sample-index tie rule for equal distances; break a class vote tie by the smallest class label. Define the squared Mahalanobis distance $q_M(x,x')=d_M^2(x,x')=(x-x')^TM(x-x')$ with $M\\succeq0$. Its nonnegative square root $d_M(x,x')=\\sqrt{q_M(x,x')}$ is the pseudometric: equivalently $M=L^TL$ and $d_M(x,x')=\\|Lx-Lx'\\|_2$; singular $M$ may identify distinct points. In LMNN, fix for each $i$ a set $N_i$ of $k$ target neighbors with $j\\ne i$ and $y_j=y_i$ (assume at least $k$ same-class candidates, or define a smaller $k_i$ in advance). Define $\\epsilon_{\\rm pull}(M)=\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}d_M^2(x_i,x_j)$ and $\\epsilon_{\\rm push}(M)=\\sum_i\\sum_{j\\in N_i}\\sum_{l:y_l\\ne y_i}[1+d_M^2(x_i,x_j)-d_M^2(x_i,x_l)]_+$. The LMNN SDP minimizes $(1-\\mu)\\epsilon_{\\rm pull}(M)+\\mu\\epsilon_{\\rm push}(M)$ over $M\\succeq0$, for fixed $\\mu\\in[0,1]$ and fixed target neighbors. Each squared distance is linear in $M$, each hinge is convex, and the PSD cone is convex, so the objective is convex. This is a training surrogate, not test error or calibrated/generalization risk.\n\nDefine a finite gradient-projection procedure for a supplied iteration count $T$, initial $M_0\\succeq0$, positive step sizes $\\eta_t$, and the above objective $F$: at step $t$, select a subgradient $G_t\\in\\partial F(M_t)$ (choose hinge subgradient $0$ at an exact kink), write $M_t-\\eta_tG_t=U_t\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_{t,j})U_t^T$ and set $M_{t+1}=U_t\\operatorname{diag}(\\max\\{\\lambda_{t,j},0\\})U_t^T$; return $M_T$. This is the Frobenius projection onto the PSD cone; no solver convergence or empirical-error improvement follows without step-size and objective-optimization analysis. **Proof route:** write $d_M^2=\\operatorname{tr}(M(x-x')(x-x')^T)$, so every pull term is linear and every push hinge is convex in $M$; intersections with $M\\succeq0$ form a convex SDP. Derive the finite subgradient and projection updates from the stated formulas; eigenvalue clipping is proved by the SVD/Frobenius projection argument if included as a theorem. **Examples:** rank-deficient $M$ gives a pseudometric; fixed targets can conflict; a projection step can reduce objective without establishing convergence. **Requires:** published `thm-non-negative-operator-characterisations` for the finite PSD matrix clause; `cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms` and `thm-spectral-and-frobenius-norms-are-unitarily-invariant-with-singular-value-formulas-and-rank-comparison` for eigenvalue clipping; `thm-projection-onto-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set` and `def-countable-choice` for existence/uniqueness of each Frobenius projection; the projection theorem uses Countable Choice, which must be carried into any theorem analyzing this procedure. The hinge objective’s convexity is proved directly from affine distances and the convex positive-part function; no C² Hessian criterion is applied. ML-14 — RKHS regularization, kernels, and support-vector machines is needed only for an optional separately defined kernelized extension. **Sources:** Weinberger and Saul, “Distance Metric Learning for Large Margin Nearest Neighbor Classification,” §2.1 and §§3.1–3.5, printed pp. 209–221 (Mahalanobis pseudometrics, target neighbors, impostors, pull/push hinge and convex SDP); §§5.3, 6.1–6.3, pp. 230–240; Appendix A.2–A.3 (custom solver). The paper’s convex formulation does not prove convergence of the custom projection solver or empirical kNN error gains.",
      "items": [
        {
