      "description": "Controlling refreshed prose: research/plan-machine-learning-track.md, complete pair section and Current published supplier reconciliation (SHA-256 348a19b0017653faf85a3708273ef33461a42a3d1dffb0e3861684b7f53fff73). Read its complete current contract and referenced prerequisite modules; preserved historical wording does not override the dated interface repair. This is prose planning, not proof acceptance.\n\n**Pages:** ml02-differential-privacy / ml02-differential-privacy-examples. **Scope:** Define neighboring databases by one record addition or removal, and a randomized mechanism as a probability kernel from the database space to a measurable output space. For every neighboring $D,D'$ and measurable output event $A$, define $(\\varepsilon,\\delta)$-DP by $\\Pr[M(D)\\in A]\\le e^\\varepsilon\\Pr[M(D')\\in A]+\\delta$, with $\\varepsilon\\ge0$, $0\\le\\delta\\le1$. Define global $\\ell_1$-sensitivity $\\Delta_1f=\\max_{D\\sim D'}\\|f(D)-f(D')\\|_1$; for $\\varepsilon>0$, the Laplace mechanism releases $f(D)+Z$ with independent coordinates $Z_j\\sim\\operatorname{Lap}(\\Delta_1f/\\varepsilon)$ and is $(\\varepsilon,0)$-DP. Define exponential-mechanism utility sensitivity $\\Delta_u=\\max_{D\\sim D',r}|u(D,r)-u(D',r)|$, require a database-independent finite range $\\mathcal R$ and $\\Delta_u>0$, and sample $r$ with probability proportional to $\\exp(\\varepsilon u(D,r)/(2\\Delta_u))$. It is $(\\varepsilon,0)$-DP; writing $\\mathcal R_{\\rm OPT}=\\{r:u(D,r)=\\max_{r'}u(D,r')\\}$, for each $t>0$,\n$$\n\\Pr\\!\\left[u(D,M(D))\\le \\operatorname{OPT}_u(D)-\\frac{2\\Delta_u}{\\varepsilon}\\left(\\log\\frac{|\\mathcal R|}{|\\mathcal R_{\\rm OPT}|}+t\\right)\\right]\\le e^{-t}.\n$$\nState postprocessing for any data-independent randomized map. For adaptive composition, if round $i$, conditional on every prior transcript, is $(\\varepsilon_i,\\delta_i)$-DP, basic composition is $(\\sum_i\\varepsilon_i,\\sum_i\\delta_i)$-DP. Assuming AC, for $k$ adaptive $(\\varepsilon,\\delta)$-DP rounds and $0<\\delta'<1$, advanced composition is $(\\varepsilon',k\\delta+\\delta')$-DP, where $\\varepsilon'=\\sqrt{2k\\log(1/\\delta')}\\,\\varepsilon+k\\varepsilon(e^\\varepsilon-1)$; state $k\\delta+\\delta'\\le1$ when a nontrivial guarantee is intended. Distinguish record-level central DP from local/client-level DP and secure aggregation; keeping records on devices alone gives no DP guarantee, and DP does not prevent inference about attributes shared by many records. Proposed items: def-differential-privacy-and-adjacency; def-global-l1-sensitivity; alg-laplace-mechanism; thm-laplace-mechanism-privacy; alg-exponential-mechanism; thm-exponential-mechanism-privacy-and-utility; thm-dp-postprocessing; thm-basic-and-advanced-composition. **Proof route:** compare Laplace output densities by the $\\ell_1$ triangle inequality; for the exponential mechanism bound both the score ratio and partition-function ratio by $e^{\\varepsilon/2}$, then sum utility mass outside the threshold; prove postprocessing by event preimages; prove basic composition on arbitrary measurable outputs by the bounded-test-function event extension. For advanced composition retain arbitrary measurable outputs: prove lem-dp-joint-measure-purification using ordinary finite-measure RN derivatives of joint measures with a fixed history marginal, then thm-advanced-adaptive-composition-arbitrary-measurable-outputs using consistent prefix laws, conditional loss drift and predictable-interval Azuma. No regular conditional distribution or jointly measurable kernel RN derivative is assumed. The standard-Borel specialization remains available. The advanced clause carries AC; the basic clause is choice-free. The general advanced supplier permits any symmetric adjacency relation on a power-set input set, including the ML-28 loss-vector replacement relation. Separate the ideal real-valued mechanism from floating-point implementations, whose rounding can destroy support and DP. **Examples:** add/remove noisy count; finite hypothesis selection; postprocessing a private output; record-level versus client-level adjacency. **Requires:** published def-measurable-space, def-probability-measure, and def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel for output events and randomized mechanisms; published def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable for privacy-loss expectations. The mechanism definitions and all adjacency/output-space hypotheses are stated locally. **Sources:** Dwork and Roth, The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy, Ch. 2 §§2.1–2.4, printed pp. 11–27 (adjacency, DP, postprocessing, group privacy and limitations); Ch. 3 §§3.2–3.5.1, pp. 29–50 (sensitivity, Laplace, exponential mechanism, simple/advanced composition and complete proofs); Ch. 3 §§3.9–3.10, pp. 58–65 (additional composition/privacy discussion). The complete relevant source chapters and proof units were read; ideal continuous-noise implementation caveats remain material.",
