          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Take the finite MDP with $S=\\{s\\}$, $A(s)=\\{a\\}$, reward alphabet $\\mathcal R=\\{1\\}$, and $p(s,1\\mid s,a)=1$. At $\\gamma=1$, the first $N$ terms of the return sum to $N$, so the undiscounted return diverges. The formal backup $F:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$, $F(v)=1+v$, has no real fixed point and is not a contraction with any constant $0\\le q<1$ in the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb R}(v,w)=|v-w|$.",
          "strategy": "Compute the finite partial sums and solve the formal fixed-point equation directly.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Richard S. Sutton and Andrew G. Barto, Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction, 2nd ed.",
                "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/class/psych209/Readings/SuttonBartoIPRLBook2ndEd.pdf",
                "locator": "§3.7, the restriction to discounted continuing returns when 0≤γ<1."
