          "id": "def-finite-pomdp",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Finite partially observable Markov decision process",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-markov-decision-process",
            "def-measure-kernel-and-probability-kernel"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "statement": "Let $S$ be a finite nonempty set of hidden states, $A$ a finite nonempty action set available in every state, and $\\Omega$ a finite nonempty observation set. Give these sets their power-set sigma-algebras. A finite POMDP with known model consists of a finite reward alphabet $\\mathcal R\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ and a joint probability mass $p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)$ on $S\\times\\mathcal R$ for each $(s,a)\\in S\\times A$, an observation probability distribution $O(\\cdot\\mid s')$ on $\\Omega$ for each $s'\\in S$, and an initial belief $b_0:S\\to[0,1]$ with $\\sum_{s\\in S}b_0(s)=1$. Put\n\n$$T(s'\\mid s,a)=\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a),\\qquad r(s,a)=\\sum_{s'\\in S}\\sum_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal R}\\rho\\,p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a).$$\n\nAt decision time $t$, write $S_t\\in S$ for the hidden state, $A_t\\in A$ for the chosen action, $R_t\\in\\mathcal R$ for the realized reward, and $O_{t+1}\\in\\Omega$ for the next observation. Conditional on $S_t=s$ and $A_t=a$, the joint law of next state $S_{t+1}=s'$, reward $R_t=\\rho$, and observation $O_{t+1}=o$ is $p(s',\\rho\\mid s,a)O(o\\mid s')$. Thus the observation depends only on the next state and is not an additional observation of the reward. A policy uses only the past actions and observations; realized rewards are not included in its history. For a horizon $H\\in\\mathbb N$ and discount $\\gamma\\in[0,1]$, the objective is to maximize $\\mathbb E[\\sum_{t=0}^{H-1}\\gamma^tR_t]$. The model is known; learning its transition, reward, or observation laws is outside this definition.",
          "strategy": "Specify a finite hidden-state MDP, an action-independent next-state observation kernel, the initial belief, observed history, and a finite-horizon objective.",
          "sources": {
