        {
          "id": "thm-belief-mdp-sufficient-statistic",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "The belief state is a sufficient statistic for finite POMDP control",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-pomdp",
            "def-belief-state-and-bayes-filter",
            "lem-finite-pomdp-belief-update-is-probability-kernel",
            "def-sum-over-a-finite-index-set",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws",
            "lem-finite-set-has-max"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 4,
          "statement": "Let a finite POMDP with known model be fixed. A deterministic history policy chooses an action from the finite action set using only preceding actions and observations; a randomized history policy assigns an action distribution using only that history, with its randomization conditionally independent of the hidden state. For every positive-probability history $h_t$ with belief $b_t$ and every action $a$ having positive conditional selection probability at that history,\n\n$$\\Pr(S_{t+1}=s',O_{t+1}=o\\mid h_t,A_t=a)=\\sum_{s\\in S}b_t(s)T(s'\\mid s,a)O(o\\mid s').$$\n\nConsequently the observation probability is $q(o\\mid b_t,a)$, the next belief is $\\tau(b_t,a,o)$ whenever that probability is positive, and the conditional expected reward is $\\bar r(b_t,a)$. The controlled belief-state model has the fully observed Borel state space $\\Delta(S)$, action set $A$, transition kernel $K$, and reward $\\bar r$; under a policy depending only on the current belief, the belief process is Markov. For every remaining finite horizon $n$, the optimal value depends on the history only through $b$ and satisfies\n\n$$V_0(b)=0,\\qquad V_{n+1}(b)=\\max_{a\\in A}\\left[\\bar r(b,a)+\\gamma\\sum_{o:q(o\\mid b,a)>0}q(o\\mid b,a)V_n(\\tau(b,a,o))\\right].$$\n\nEach maximum is attained; randomizing an action cannot increase the value. An observation with zero probability requires no posterior update. The conclusion holds for $\\gamma\\in[0,1]$ and uses no Axiom of Choice.",
          "strategy": "Factor the next-state/observation law through the current conditional state distribution, then induct on the remaining finite horizon using the finite action maximum.",
          "proof_strategy": "direct",
          "sources": {
