            "thm-induction-principle"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 5,
          "statement": "Fix a finite POMDP, horizon $H\\in\\mathbb N$, and discount $\\gamma\\in[0,1]$. For $0\\le h\\le H$, let $\\Gamma_h$ be the finite alpha-vector set returned by the exhaustive backup, with set semantics for duplicate vectors. Then, for every belief $b\\in\\Delta(S)$,\n\n$$V_h(b)=\\max_{\\alpha\\in\\Gamma_h}\\sum_{s\\in S}b(s)\\alpha(s),$$\n\nwhere $V_h$ is the optimal expected $h$-step discounted reward. Thus $V_h$ is the upper envelope of finitely many linear functions on $\\Delta(S)$: it is linear on each region $R_\\alpha=\\{b\\in\\Delta(S):\\sum_s b(s)\\alpha(s)\\ge\\sum_s b(s)\\beta(s)\\text{ for every }\\beta\\in\\Gamma_h\\}$ and is convex. Define a **witness belief** for $\\alpha\\in\\Gamma_h$ to be any $b\\in\\Delta(S)$ with $\\sum_s b(s)\\alpha(s)=V_h(b)$. Removing every vector with no witness belief leaves $V_h$ unchanged. For the count, $\\mathcal P_0$ contains the empty continuation and each $\\mathcal P_{h+1}$ consists of a root action and one depth-$h$ continuation for each observation; with $N_h=|\\mathcal P_h|$, $N_0=1$ and $N_{h+1}=|A|N_h^{|\\Omega|}$. The exhaustive candidate and witness-vector counts are bounded by $N_h$, which can grow exponentially in $h$. No infinite-horizon representation or polynomial-time pruning claim is made.",
          "strategy": "Induct on horizon to identify each policy tree's state-value vector, average under the initial belief, take the finite maximum, and prove envelope convexity and witness-pruning invariance.",
