          "dependency_level": 4,
          "statement": "Let $S=\\{s_0,s_1\\}$, $A=\\{a\\}$, $\\Omega=\\{x,y\\}$, reward alphabet $\\{0\\}$, horizon $H=1$, and discount $\\gamma=1$. From each state, the joint next-state/reward law assigns probability one to that same state with reward zero. Put $b_0(s_0)=1$, $b_0(s_1)=0$, and let $O(x\\mid s_0)=1$, $O(y\\mid s_0)=0$, $O(x\\mid s_1)=0$, and $O(y\\mid s_1)=1$. Then the observation distribution has $q(y\\mid b_0,a)=0$ and $q(x\\mid b_0,a)=1$, so $\\tau(b_0,a,y)$ is undefined and the belief transition uses only the positive-mass $x$ branch.",
