        {
          "id": "ex-pomdp-policy-tree-enumeration-grows-exponentially",
          "kind": "example",
          "title": "A finite POMDP family has exponentially many witnessed alpha vectors",
          "deps": [
            "def-finite-pomdp",
            "def-belief-state-and-bayes-filter",
            "alg-pomdp-witness-backup",
            "thm-finite-horizon-pomdp-piecewise-linear-convex-value",
            "def-finite-cardinality",
            "def-nat-power",
            "thm-product-rule",
            "thm-cardinality-of-a-set-of-functions",
            "def-finite-sum",
            "lem-finite-sum-laws",
            "def-natural-numbers",
            "def-nat-order",
            "def-nat-addition",
            "lem-nat-order-is-membership",
            "lem-nat-transitive-irreflexive",
            "thm-induction-principle",
            "lem-cardinality-of-finite-natural-initial-segment"
          ],
          "dependency_level": 6,
          "statement": "For each $n\\in\\mathbb N$ with $0<n$, let $I_n=\\{t\\in\\mathbb N:t<n\\}$ be a clock set and let a hidden binary string be a function $x:I_n\\to\\{0,1\\}$. Write $\\sigma(t)$ for the natural successor. Put $S=\\{(x,t):x\\in\\{0,1\\}^{I_n},\\ t\\in I_n\\}$, $A=\\{0,1\\}$, $\\Omega=\\{o\\}$, and reward alphabet $\\mathcal R=\\{0,1\\}$. For $t\\in I_n$, define $t^+=\\sigma(t)$ if $\\sigma(t)\\in I_n$, and $t^+=t$ otherwise. Under action $a$ at $(x,t)$, the joint next-state/reward law assigns probability one to next state $(x,t^+)$ and reward $1_{\\{a=x(t)\\}}$; the sole observation $o$ occurs with probability one. Let $x^\\circ:I_n\\to\\{0,1\\}$ be the constant-zero string, take $b_0=\\delta_{(x^\\circ,0)}$, and set horizon $H=n$ and $\\gamma=1$. There are exactly $2^n$ depth-$n$ policy trees, one for each binary action string $y:I_n\\to\\{0,1\\}$. The alpha vector of tree $y$, evaluated at state $(x,0)$, is $\\sum_{t<n}1_{\\{x(t)=y(t)\\}}$. At the point-mass belief $\\delta_{(y,0)}$ this vector has value $n$, while every distinct action string has value strictly less than $n$. Therefore all $2^n$ alpha vectors are distinct and each has a witness belief in $\\Delta(S)$, which need not be reachable from the specified initial belief; the family has state count $n2^n$.",
