{
      "order": 2996,
      "id": "semi-supervised-graph-propagation-and-pseudolabeling-limits",
      "title": "Semi-supervised graph propagation and pseudo-labeling limits",
      "kind": "A",
      "category": "machine-learning",
      "label": "ML-93",
      "companion": "semi-supervised-graph-propagation-and-pseudolabeling-limits-examples",
      "requires": [
        "ml02-spectral-clustering",
        "prediction-problems-risks-and-empirical-minimization"
      ],
      "description": "Controlling refreshed prose: research/plan-machine-learning-track.md, complete pair section and Current published supplier reconciliation (SHA-256 348a19b0017653faf85a3708273ef33461a42a3d1dffb0e3861684b7f53fff73). Read its complete current contract and referenced prerequisite modules; preserved historical wording does not override the dated interface repair. This is prose planning, not proof acceptance.\n\n**Pages:** semi-supervised-graph-propagation-and-pseudolabeling-limits / semi-supervised-graph-propagation-and-pseudolabeling-limits-examples. **Scope and interfaces:** `def-graph-harmonic-label-extension`, `alg-graph-harmonic-label-propagation`, `thm-graph-harmonic-extension-uniqueness-and-maximum-principle`, `alg-finite-round-confidence-pseudolabeling`, `thm-unlabeled-law-alone-does-not-identify-labels`, `rem-semisupervised-cluster-assumptions`. Let W be a finite symmetric nonnegative weight matrix, L=D−W, nonempty labeled subset B, unlabeled subset U and prescribed real labels y_B. Require every connected component containing an unlabeled vertex to meet B; an all-unlabeled component returns `unanchored-component` instead of a singular solve. Minimize E(f)=one-half f^T L f with f_B=y_B. The unique unlabeled solution is f_U=−L_UU^{-1}L_UB y_B. The algorithm validates symmetry/nonnegativity and component anchors, forms the principal linear system, calls an SPD solver with finite budget/residual and returns values/status. Multiclass one-hot labels can be propagated one coordinate at a time; each predicted row lies in the probability simplex under the same exact anchored solve. A residual certificate is numerical; it does not certify a true class label.\n\n**Argument:** For a vector v zero on B, v^T L v=one-half sum_ij w_ij(v_i−v_j)^2. It vanishes only if v is constant on every component; an anchor forces each relevant constant to zero. Thus L_UU is SPD, giving existence/uniqueness and the normal equations. Each unlabeled value equals a nonnegative weighted average of its neighbors. A strict interior maximum above the boundary maximum propagates to every connected neighbor, eventually contradicting an anchor; the minimum is analogous. Apply this to each one-hot coordinate and to their sum, whose unique harmonic boundary data are one, to obtain nonnegative coordinates summing to one. Pseudo-labeling is a separate heuristic: for T finite rounds retrain with a stated learner on true labels plus selected high-confidence predictions, specify threshold, no-relabel rule/ties, weights and stop when no points are added. Finite termination follows from the supplied budget/no-relabel rule, not from improved test risk. Without a structural link between P_X and P_(Y|X), two laws can have identical P_X, identical labels on every observed labeled input and opposite deterministic labels at an unobserved input of positive P_X mass. Any same output predictor has error at least half that unseen mass under one of the two laws. This elementary two-point experiment proves nonidentification; it does not refute gains under explicit cluster/smoothness/low-density-separation assumptions.\n\n**Prerequisites:** ML-20 weighted-Laplacian identity, published SPD solves, ML-01 risk. **Examples:** path with endpoint labels; unanchored component; confident wrong pseudo-label locked in by the no-relabel rule; indistinguishable unlabeled laws. **Sources:** the weighted graph and finite decision-theoretic suppliers already specified in ML-20/MSF-03; harmonic-extension uniqueness, maximum principle and nonidentification have complete supporting arguments above. No continuum graph-limit, semi-supervised minimax improvement or consistency theorem is imported without a separate sampling/scaling/prerequisite design.",
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "alg-finite-round-confidence-pseudolabeling",
          "kind": "algorithm",
          "title": "Finite-round confidence-threshold pseudo-labeling",
          "statement": "Let $q\\ge2$, let $\\mathcal C=\\{1,\\ldots,q\\}$, and let $(\\mathcal X,d)$ be a finite metric space, meaning $d:\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal X\\to[0,\\infty)$ is symmetric, $d(x,y)=0$ iff $x=y$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$ for all $x,y,z$. The input is a nonempty ordered list $S_0=((x_i,y_i))_{i=1}^m$ of labeled examples with $y_i\\in\\mathcal C$, a finite ordered set $U_0\\subseteq\\mathcal X\\setminus\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ of unlabeled examples, an integer $k$ with $1\\le k\\le m$, a confidence threshold $\\tau\\in[1/q,1]$, a pseudo-label weight $\\lambda>0$, and a round cap $T\\in\\mathbb N_0$. A true labeled record has weight $1$; a pseudo-labeled record has weight $\\lambda$.\n\nMaintain an ordered training list $R_t$, initially $S_0$, a remaining unlabeled set $U_t$, initially $U_0$, and an initially empty pseudo-label log. To predict at $x\\in U_t$, order all records $(x_i,y_i,w_i)\\in R_t$ by increasing $(d(x,x_i),i)$, where $i$ is their stable record index, and take the first $k$ records $N_t(x)$. For $c\\in\\mathcal C$, put $V_{t,c}(x)=\\sum_{(x_i,y_i,w_i)\\in N_t(x)}w_i\\mathbf 1\\{y_i=c\\}$. Let $\\widehat y_t(x)$ be the smallest class index attaining $\\max_c V_{t,c}(x)$, and define confidence $\\kappa_t(x)=\\max_c V_{t,c}(x)/\\sum_cV_{t,c}(x)$. The denominator is positive because $k\\ge1$ and every record weight is positive.\n\nIf $U_0=\\varnothing$, set status $\\texttt{no-unlabeled-inputs}$ and skip the round loop. Otherwise, for rounds $t=0,\\ldots,T-1$, compute all predictions from the same current list $R_t$. Let $A_t=\\{x\\in U_t:\\kappa_t(x)\\ge\\tau\\}$. If $A_t=\\varnothing$, set status $\\texttt{no-additions}$ and leave the loop. Otherwise append one pseudo-labeled record for each $x\\in A_t$, in the supplied order on $U_0$, using its predicted class and weight $\\lambda$; append $(t,x,\\widehat y_t(x),\\lambda)$ to the pseudo-label log, and remove all points in $A_t$ from $U_t$. A pseudo-label is never revised. If the updated $U_t=\\varnothing$, set status $\\texttt{all-labelled}$ and leave the loop. If all $T$ rounds finish without an earlier stop and points remain in $U_t$, set status $\\texttt{round-cap}$; this includes the case $T=0$.\n\nFor any query $x\\in\\mathcal X$, define the returned classifier by the same nearest-record ordering and weighted vote using the final training list, with the smallest-index vote tie rule. In every case return that classifier, the pseudo-label log, the remaining unlabeled set, and the status. The procedure makes no population-risk or pseudo-label-correctness guarantee.",
          "deps": [],
          "justified_by": [],
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "strategy": "Definition: specify the finite weighted k-nearest-neighbor learner, deterministic vote and distance ties, confidence, threshold, simultaneous updates, round cap, stable ordering, and no-relabel rule.",
          "sources": {
