        {
          "id": "def-graph-harmonic-label-extension",
          "kind": "definition",
          "title": "Graph-harmonic label extension on a finite weighted graph",
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, $V=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, and let $W=(w_{ij})\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be symmetric, entrywise nonnegative, and zero-diagonal. Put $d_i=\\sum_jw_{ij}$, $D=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\ldots,d_n)$, and $L=D-W$. The support graph has edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $w_{ij}>0$; two vertices lie in the same connected component exactly when a finite path of support edges joins them; connected components include isolated singleton vertices. Let $B\\subseteq V$ be nonempty, put $U=V\\setminus B$, and prescribe $y_B\\in\\mathbb R^B$. Call the boundary anchored when every connected component meeting $U$ also meets $B$. For $f\\in\\mathbb R^V$ with $f|_B=y_B$, define the Dirichlet graph energy $E(f)=\\tfrac12 f^TLf$. A function is harmonic on $U$ when $(Lf)_u=0$ for every $u\\in U$. A graph-harmonic label extension is any minimizer of $E$ over the affine set of functions with the prescribed boundary values; the definition itself makes no uniqueness assertion.",
          "deps": [
