        {
          "id": "thm-unlabeled-law-alone-does-not-identify-labels",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "The unlabeled marginal alone does not identify labels",
          "statement": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty feature set with its discrete sigma-algebra, and let $\\mu:\\mathcal X\\to[0,1]$ satisfy $\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\mu(x)=1$. Let $\\mathcal S\\subsetneq\\mathcal X$ be a nonempty set of observed labeled inputs, and choose $x_\\star\\in\\mathcal X\\setminus\\mathcal S$ with $q=\\mu(x_\\star)>0$. Let $h_0,h_1:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}$ agree on $\\mathcal X\\setminus\\{x_\\star\\}$ and differ at $x_\\star$. For $j\\in\\{0,1\\}$, let $P_j$ be the joint law assigning mass $\\mu(x)$ to $(x,h_j(x))$. Both laws have the same feature marginal and the same observed labels on $\\mathcal S$. Let $\\mathcal H=\\{g:\\mathcal X\\to\\{0,1\\}\\}$, and let $Q:\\mathcal H\\to[0,1]$ satisfy $\\sum_{g\\in\\mathcal H}Q(g)=1$, so it is a probability vector; take the same $Q$ under both laws, as a predictor rule using their common information must do. For the zero-one population risk\n$$R_{P_j}(g)=\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}\\mu(x)\\mathbf 1\\{g(x)\\ne h_j(x)\\},$$\nthe expected risks satisfy\n$$\\max_{j\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\sum_{g\\in\\mathcal H}Q(g)R_{P_j}(g)\\ge\\frac q2.$$",
          "deps": [
            "def-supervised-learning-problem",
            "def-population-and-empirical-risk"
          ],
          "justified_by": [],
