          "statement": "Let $n\\ge1$, $V=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, and let $W=(w_{ij})\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be symmetric, entrywise nonnegative, and zero-diagonal. Put $d_i=\\sum_jw_{ij}$, $D=\\operatorname{diag}(d_1,\\ldots,d_n)$, and $L=D-W$. The support graph has edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $w_{ij}>0$. Two vertices lie in the same connected component exactly when a finite path of support edges joins them. Let $B\\subseteq V$ be nonempty, $U=V\\setminus B$, and prescribe $y_B\\in\\mathbb R^B$. For index sets $S,T\\subseteq V$, let $L_{ST}$ denote the submatrix with rows in $S$ and columns in $T$, in increasing index order. Assume every support-graph component meeting $U$ also meets $B$. Then the energy $E(f)=\\tfrac12 f^TLf$, minimized over $f|_B=y_B$, has exactly one minimizer. If $U=\\varnothing$, it is $f=y_B$. If $U\\ne\\varnothing$, the principal block $L_{UU}$ is real symmetric positive definite, meaning $v^TL_{UU}v>0$ for every nonzero $v\\in\\mathbb R^U$, and the minimizer is $f_U=-L_{UU}^{-1}L_{UB}y_B$. Each $u\\in U$ has $d_u>0$ and $f_u=\\sum_jw_{uj}f_j/d_u$; hence $\\min_{b\\in B}y_b\\le f_u\\le\\max_{b\\in B}y_b$. For $C\\ge2$, if each boundary value is one-hot (one coordinate equals 1 and all others equal 0) in $\\mathbb R^C$, the coordinatewise energy minimizers have every row in the probability simplex $\\Delta_C=\\{p\\in[0,1]^C:\\sum_{c=1}^Cp_c=1\\}$.",
