          "id": "rem-semisupervised-cluster-assumptions",
          "kind": "remark",
          "title": "Structural assumptions behind semi-supervised gains",
          "statement": "Here semi-supervised input means labeled examples together with unlabeled examples or their feature marginal $P_X$. The finite harmonic extension uses the supplied graph weights to prefer scores that vary little across large-weight edges; it does not identify the ground-truth conditional label law $P(Y\\mid X)$. A cluster assumption posits that points in the same cluster are likely to share a class; low-density separation posits that a classifier boundary lies in a region of small $P_X$-mass; graph smoothness favors scores that vary little across edges with large supplied weights. These are additional modeling assumptions relating $P_X$ and $P(Y\\mid X)$, not consequences of observing $P_X$. The pseudo-label procedure is also a training heuristic. This batch states no general population-risk improvement, consistency, minimax, calibration, or graph-limit theorem. The exact harmonic maximum principle concerns the supplied boundary labels, not unknown true labels.",
